course: world
lesson: 175
title: "It's on TikTok so it's true" and the comment section
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ollie sees a TikTok saying a phone charges if you put it in the microwave for ten seconds. "It's on TikTok, so it must be true." His mum grabs the phone: "No! That's how you get a fire." Anyone can post anything, and some videos are pranks made to trick you.
explain: Online, attention is easy to fake. Views and likes measure how many people watched or clicked, not whether something is true. A comment liked 3,000 times ("my uncle works there") is still just one stranger's say-so.
explain: Look out for the tricks: a shocking title made for clicks ("Scientists SHOCKED: chocolate makes you smarter!"), an account called "TotallyNotABot123", a fake "famous singer has died" story, a cat clip edited to say "hello", and comments that insult instead of giving reasons.
explain: Before believing or sharing, ask: who posted it, can I check it somewhere trusted, and is there another explanation? The "waving" flag in the Moon landing video wobbled because astronauts twisted its pole, and with no air it kept wobbling longer.
explain: People take the mickey out of the click, not the clicker: "Scientists shocked? The only one shocked is your dentist."
fact: Anyone can post anything online, so being on TikTok or YouTube doesn't make something true.
fact: Never put a phone or anything metal in a microwave: it can spark and catch fire.
fact: Views and likes measure attention, not truth; grown-ups call "lots of people liked it, so it's true" the bandwagon.
fact: "My uncle works there" in a comment is hearsay: a claim nobody can check.
fact: Big news, like a famous person dying, should be checked on a trusted news website before you believe or share it; fake death stories spread a lot online.
fact: Calling people names instead of giving reasons is called attacking the person; it is not an argument.
fact: Cats make many sounds, and one may sound like a word to us, but cats don't speak English; amazing claims need strong proof.
fact: Official game news comes from the company's own website or channel, not from random accounts with no picture.
fact: Toothpaste can irritate skin, and doctors don't recommend it for spots.
fact: Clickbait is a title made to get clicks by stretching or twisting what was found; studies on cocoa found at most small effects, nothing like "chocolate makes you smarter".
fact: Astronauts walked on the Moon between 1969 and 1972 and left mirrors there that scientists still bounce lasers off.
fact: The Moon flag had a rod along its top to hold it out; it moved when astronauts twisted the pole, and with no air to slow it, it kept wobbling longer.
fact: Words people use: "a bot" is a fake computer account, "a life hack" is a clever trick for everyday life, "clickbait" is a title made to make you click, "no cap" means honestly, "a tantrum with a keyboard" means an angry online rant, and "mrow" is a cat's sound.
exercise: Ollie sees a TikTok saying a microwave charges a phone. What is true? | Putting a phone in a microwave can cause sparks and fire | It charges the battery fully in ten seconds, like a fast charger | It only works for old phones with removable batteries | It is safe as long as you stand and watch it closely
exercise: Ollie believes it because it's on TikTok. What is the broken step? | Anyone can post anything; being online doesn't prove it | TikTok checks every single video for the truth before it goes up | Funny videos are always true, because the jokers check their facts | Short videos can't lie, because there isn't time to make things up
exercise: Ruby sees a comment, "this is fake, my uncle works there", with 3,000 likes. What is it really? | One stranger's claim that nobody can check | Proof, because 3,000 people checked it and agreed | An official statement from the company itself | A news report written by a careful journalist
exercise: Ellie tells everyone a famous singer has died. What happened next day? | The singer posted a new video | The news was confirmed | The singer's family thanked her | Nobody ever found out
exercise: What should Ellie have done before sharing the news? | Checked a trusted news website | Told only her best friends, so it wouldn't spread | Waited until the post got more likes | Asked the comment section
exercise: A comment says, "If you disagree with me you're stupid." What kind of argument is it? | Not an argument: it attacks people instead of giving reasons | A strong argument with good reasons | A fair point Sam should accept | Evidence that Sam is wrong
exercise: Kai sees a cat saying "hello" in English. What is most likely? | A cat sound that happened to sound like a word, or an edit | This cat really learned English words from listening to its owner | Cats can speak English if they hear it every day | Only some special cats can talk, and this is one
exercise: Leo reads that a game will be free next week, from "TotallyNotABot123". Where should real game news come from? | The game company's own website or channel | Any comment that has lots and lots of likes | Accounts with funny names, because they're insiders | Whoever posts the news first
exercise: Priya rubs toothpaste on a spot because the hack has millions of views. What happens? | Her skin gets red and sore | The spot vanishes overnight | Her skin becomes smoother | Nothing happens at all
exercise: What do views measure? | How many people watched, not whether it's true | Whether doctors approve | Whether the video is correct | How many experts agree
exercise: Jack stops playing a game he likes because of a mean comment. What is true? | An insult from a stranger is just noise, not a reason | The comment proves the game is really bad, so he should stop | Everyone who likes that game is a loser, so he must quit | He should delete all his games so nobody can be mean
exercise: Ava eats a whole chocolate bar after a "Scientists SHOCKED" video. What happened in her test? | She got the same mark as usual | She got full marks for the very first time | She got a much lower mark because of the sugar | The test was cancelled
exercise: What is clickbait? | A title made to get clicks by stretching what was found | A careful summary of a study | A kind of fishing game | A safety warning on a video
exercise: Noah believes the Moon flag "waving" proves the landing was fake. Why did the flag move? | Astronauts twisted the pole, and with no air it wobbled longer | Wind on the Moon blew it | It was filmed on Earth in the wind | Flags always wave by themselves
exercise: What did astronauts leave on the Moon that scientists still use? | Mirrors that lasers bounce off | Working cameras that film Earth | A flag that still waves | Radios that still send messages
test: His mum: "No! That's how you get a fire, Ollie. Not everything on TikTok is a life hack." What is a "life hack"? | A clever trick for everyday life | A way to hack into other people's phones | A dangerous dare people do for views | A type of battery that never runs out
test: Ruby's brother: "Everybody's uncle works at Nintendo in the comments. Nintendo must be massive." What is he mocking? | That a comment like that is proof | Nintendo, for being such a big company | People who have uncles with jobs in games | Ruby, for reading the comments at all
test: Friend: "She posted a dance video, Ellie. For someone dead, she's got really good moves." What is the lesson? | Check big news before sharing it | Dead people can post videos | Dance videos are always fake | Never talk about singers
test: Sam's mum: "That's not an argument, love, that's a tantrum with a keyboard." What should Sam do? | Look for reasons, and feel free to disagree | Agree with the comment, so he isn't called stupid | Reply with an even worse insult to win the fight | Stop using the internet forever and never go back
test: Kai's sister: "Your cat says 'mrow' and wants food. That's the full vocabulary, Kai." What is she saying? | His cat makes cat sounds, not English words | His cat can say more words than the video cat | Cats only speak when hungry | Cats speak English to their owners
test: His mate: "TotallyNotABot123, Leo? With no profile picture? Come on, bro." What does "a bot" mean? | A fake computer account | A famous gamer with a secret name | A game company's official account | A very fast player
test: Her aunt, a nurse: "Millions of views means millions of people watched it. Not that it works, darling." What would a doctor say about toothpaste on spots? | It can irritate skin and isn't recommended | It works best overnight | It's better than any cream | It's fine on any skin
test: His dad: "Some random person typed a word, and now you don't like your game? He doesn't even know you, son." What should Jack decide? | Liking a game is his own choice | Whatever the comments say | Whatever gets the most likes | Whatever the meanest person says
test: Ava's brother: "Scientists shocked? The only one shocked is your dentist." What did real studies find about cocoa? | At most small effects, not "chocolate makes you smarter" | Chocolate doubles your test marks if you eat a whole bar first | Chocolate makes you forget things | Scientists never studied cocoa
test: His science teacher: "The flag had a rod along the top to hold it out." Why did it keep wobbling? | There's no air on the Moon to slow it down | There's strong wind on the Moon that keeps blowing | The rod was broken, so the flag kept flapping | The astronauts kept blowing on it for the camera
test: Rough comment: "Ur thick if u believe the Moon landing, no cap." What is the best reply? | Ask for reasons, and point to the mirrors still used today | Agree, so nobody calls you thick | Reply with a worse insult | Delete your account
exam: Identify the principal error in concluding that a claim is true because it appears in a widely viewed video. | Views measure attention rather than truth | The video was insufficiently short | The video lacked music | The claim was made by a stranger with a profile picture
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the placing of a mobile telephone within a microwave oven. | It may produce sparks and cause a fire | It charges the battery within ten seconds | It improves the reception of the telephone | It is safe if undertaken briefly
exam: Determine the most appropriate course of action upon encountering a report of the death of a famous person on social media. | Verification by means of a trusted news website before sharing | Immediate distribution to all acquaintances before others share it | Acceptance if the post has received many likes | Reliance upon the comment section
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the movement of the flag in film of the Moon landings? | It was caused by astronauts twisting the pole and prolonged by the absence of air | It was caused by wind upon the Moon | It proves the filming took place on Earth | It was caused by the rocket's engines
